    "message": {
     "content": "This is an **exceptionally strong, top-tier offer**\u2014well into the 95th+ percentile for a Google L6 Senior Product Manager. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Offer Breakdown & Comparison\n\n#### **Your Meta IC6 Comp:**\n* **Base:** $240,000\n* **Target Bonus:** $36,000 (15%)\n* **RSU run-rate:** ~$250,000/yr ($200,000 over next 12 mos)\n* **Total Annualized Comp:** **~$526,000** ($476,000 over the next 12 months)\n\n---\n\n#### **Google L6 Offer:**\n* **Base:** $260,000 *(near the very top of the $183k\u2013$271k band)*\n* **Target Bonus:** $52,000 (20%)\n* **Sign-on Bonus:** $200,000 *(unusually high for L6 without a bidding war)*\n* **New-Hire GSUs:** $1,340,000 over 4 years ($335,000/yr straight-line average)\n\n#### **Vesting Structure:**\nGoogle generally uses a front-loaded vesting schedule for new-hire equity grants:\n* **Year 1 (33% to 38%):** ~$442,000 \u2013 $509,000\n* **Year 2 (32% to 33%):** ~$429,000 \u2013 $442,000\n* **Year 3 (20% to 22%):** ~$268,000 \u2013 $295,000\n* **Year 4 (10% to 12%):** ~$134,000 \u2013 $161,000\n\n#### **Total Compensation Comparison:**\n* **Google Year 1 Total:** **~$954,000 \u2013 $1,021,000** *(Base + Bonus + Sign-on + Year 1 GSUs)*\n* **Google 4-Year Average (excl. sign-on):** **~$647,000/year**\n* **Google 4-Year Average (incl. sign-on):** **~$697,000/year**\n\n---\n\n### 2. Market Benchmark Assessment\n\n* Typical L6 Senior PM compensation at Google in the Bay Area sits between **$450,000 and $550,000/year**.\n* Standard initial equity grants for L6 are typically **$600k \u2013 $800k**. An initial grant of **$1.34M** is what is typically seen for L7 (Principal PM / Director tracks) or highly contested counter-offers.\n* A **$200,000 sign-on bonus** is top-of-market and covers your unvested Meta RSUs for the next 12 months dollar-for-dollar.\n\nGoogle's compensation committee has already preemptively priced this offer to buy out your Meta equity and eliminate any hesitation.\n\n---\n\n### 3. Should You Negotiate, Accept, or Walk Away?\n\n* **Walk Away?** Absolutely not. Google is your top choice, the team is exciting, and the offer represents a ~**30%\u201335% jump in 4-year annualized earnings** and nearly **double your Year 1 take-home**.\n* **Aggressive Hardball Negotiation?** **Not recommended.** You do not have competing offers, your base is already at the 88th percentile of the posted salary band, and your equity/sign-on are already at the ceiling. Pushing too hard without leverage can create friction with leadership or compensation committees.\n* **Option A: Accept Enthusiastically (Recommended)**\n  * Since this offer is already near maximum band capacity across every lever, signing with enthusiasm sets an excellent initial relationship with your hiring manager.\n* **Option B: Soft, Low-Risk Ask (If you must negotiate)**\n  * If you want to leave no money on the table, you can make one single, polite, non-demanding ask focused on equity or base salary, tying it to a quick close.\n\n---\n\n### 4. How to Negotiate (If You Choose Option B)\n\nFocus on closing the gap on base to the top of the band or rounding up equity, while explicitly stating you will sign immediately if approved.\n\n#### **Script / Framing:**\n> *\"Thank you so much for putting together such a compelling package. Google is my absolute top choice, and I\u2019m thrilled about the team and what we\u2019ll be building. I want to confirm that if we can finalize the details, I am ready to sign immediately.*\n>\n> *Given my current trajectory at Meta and my commitment to hit the ground running, would it be possible to round the equity grant to **$1.45M** (or increase base salary to **$270,000** to reach the top of the band)? If we can make that adjustment, I\u2019ll sign the offer letter today.\"*\n\n---\n\n### 5. Key Details to Verify Before Signing\n\n1. **Sign-on Bonus Terms:** Clarify if the $200k sign-on is paid as a lump sum in your first 30 days and check the clawback timeline (standard is prorated over 12 or 24 months).\n2. **Start Date vs. Meta Vesting:** Time your resignation to capture any imminent Meta quarterly vesting or bonus payout dates prior to starting.\n3. **Refresher Expectations:** Because of front-loaded vesting (38/32/20/10), your Year 3 and Year 4 initial equity vest drops significantly. Google uses annual refreshers to fill this gap, but refreshers depend on performance ratings and Alphabet share price. Plan your personal cash-flow projections assuming the base/bonus floor rather than the Year 1 peak.",
